-- Q1 [Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs · easy · Skill 1 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans A] (g33)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs',
    $qt33$Four substances are each dissolved separately in pure water. By the Arrhenius definition, which one is an acid because dissolving it raises \([\text{H}^+]\) (i.e., \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\)) above its value in pure water?$qt33$,
    $op33$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{HCl}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{NaOH}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{NaCl}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{C}_6\\text{H}_{12}\\text{O}_6\\) (glucose)"}]$op33$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex33$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is A because the Arrhenius definition of an acid is a substance that increases the concentration of hydrogen ions, \([\text{H}^+]\) (equivalently \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\)), when dissolved in water. Hydrogen chloride dissociates essentially completely in water, \(\text{HCl} \rightarrow \text{H}^+ + \text{Cl}^-\), so it releases \(\text{H}^+\) and drives \([\text{H}^+]\) above the \(10^{-7}\ \text{M}\) value of pure water, lowering the \(\text{pH}\). That is exactly the behavior the Arrhenius definition labels an acid. (Choice B) \(\text{NaOH}\) dissolves to give \(\text{Na}^+\) and \(\text{OH}^-\); by raising the hydroxide concentration it is the Arrhenius definition of a base, not an acid, and it lowers \([\text{H}^+]\) rather than raising it. (Choice C) \(\text{NaCl}\) is the salt of a strong acid and a strong base; neither \(\text{Na}^+\) nor \(\text{Cl}^-\) hydrolyzes, so the solution stays neutral and \([\text{H}^+]\) is unchanged from pure water, making it neither an Arrhenius acid nor base. (Choice D) Glucose is a nonelectrolyte that dissolves as intact neutral molecules without releasing any ions, so it does not change \([\text{H}^+]\) and cannot be an Arrhenius acid. Skill 1$ex33$,
    'easy', '5A', $cc33$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc33$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a33B$Identifying a strong base (raises [OH-], lowers [H+]) as the acid by reversing the acid/base relationship$a33B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a33C$Treating a neutral salt of a strong acid and strong base as an acid because it dissociates into ions$a33C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a33D$Mistaking a soluble nonelectrolyte that dissolves but does not ionize for an acid$a33D$ FROM q;

-- Q2 [Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs · easy · Skill 1 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans B] (g34)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs',
    $qt34$A chemistry student dissolves ammonia (\(\text{NH}_3\)) in water and observes that the resulting solution turns red litmus blue, a hallmark of a basic solution. The student is puzzled because the formula \(\text{NH}_3\) contains no hydroxide (\(\text{OH}^-\)) group, so the Arrhenius framework offers no way to classify it as a base, even though a broader framework does. Which statement correctly explains why the Arrhenius definition fails here while a broader definition succeeds?$qt34$,
    $op34$[{"label":"A","text":"The litmus result is misleading and \\(\\text{NH}_3\\) is not actually basic; because its formula contains no \\(\\text{OH}^-\\), neither the Arrhenius nor any broader definition can treat \\(\\text{NH}_3\\) as a base."},{"label":"B","text":"The Arrhenius definition recognizes a base only if it dissociates to release \\(\\text{OH}^-\\) ions, and \\(\\text{NH}_3\\) has no \\(\\text{OH}^-\\) to release; the broader Brønsted–Lowry definition classifies \\(\\text{NH}_3\\) as a base because it accepts a proton from water, generating \\(\\text{NH}_4^+\\) and \\(\\text{OH}^-\\)."},{"label":"C","text":"The Arrhenius definition does in fact classify \\(\\text{NH}_3\\) as a base, because the nitrogen atom carries a lone pair of electrons that the definition counts as the source of basicity."},{"label":"D","text":"The solution is basic only because the dissolved water itself supplies \\(\\text{OH}^-\\), so the basic character has nothing to do with the \\(\\text{NH}_3\\) and no acid–base definition needs to invoke the \\(\\text{NH}_3\\) at all."}]$op34$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex34$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is B because the Arrhenius model is restricted to substances that, when dissolved in water, dissociate to release \(\text{OH}^-\) (for a base) or \(\text{H}^+\) (for an acid). Ammonia contains no \(\text{OH}^-\) group within its formula, so the Arrhenius framework has no mechanism to label it a base, even though the solution is observably basic. The broader Brønsted–Lowry definition removes this limitation by defining a base as a proton acceptor: \(\text{NH}_3\) accepts a proton from a water molecule (\(\text{NH}_3 + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{NH}_4^+ + \text{OH}^-\)), and the \(\text{OH}^-\) liberated from the water is what raises the pH and turns litmus blue. (Choice A) Ammonia is genuinely basic; the litmus test, the elevated \([\text{OH}^-]\), and the measured pH all confirm this, so the premise that it is not actually a base is false. (Choice C) Recognizing basicity from a lone pair of electrons is the criterion of the Lewis definition, not the Arrhenius definition; the Arrhenius framework says nothing about lone pairs and cannot classify \(\text{NH}_3\) at all. (Choice D) Pure water is neutral, with equal \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\) and \([\text{OH}^-]\); the excess \(\text{OH}^-\) that makes the solution basic arises specifically because \(\text{NH}_3\) accepts protons from water and shifts that balance, so the \(\text{NH}_3\) is the cause of the basicity. Skill 1$ex34$,
    'easy', '5A', $cc34$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc34$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a34A$denying an observed property because the expected functional group is absent from the formula$a34A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a34C$attributing the lone-pair (Lewis) criterion to the Arrhenius definition$a34C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a34D$crediting the solvent rather than the solute for the solution's acid-base character$a34D$ FROM q;

-- Q3 [Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs · medium · Skill 4 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans D] (g35)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs',
    $qt35$When formic acid and cyanide ion are combined in aqueous solution, the proton-transfer reaction \(\text{HCOOH} + \text{CN}^- \rightarrow \text{HCOO}^- + \text{HCN}\) takes place, and monitoring of the mixture shows formate \((\text{HCOO}^-)\) and \(\text{HCN}\) steadily accumulating. Which assignment of the Brønsted–Lowry acid and the Brønsted–Lowry base among the two reactants is consistent with this observed proton transfer?$qt35$,
    $op35$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{HCOOH}\\) is the base, because its more electronegative oxygen makes it the proton acceptor, and \\(\\text{CN}^-\\) is the acid that supplies the proton."},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{CN}^-\\) is the acid, donating a proton to become \\(\\text{HCN}\\), and \\(\\text{HCOOH}\\) is the base, accepting that proton to become \\(\\text{HCOO}^-\\)."},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{CN}^-\\) is the acid, because it ends up bearing the transferred proton as \\(\\text{HCN}\\), and \\(\\text{HCOOH}\\) is the base it took the proton from."},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{HCOOH}\\) is the acid, donating a proton to become \\(\\text{HCOO}^-\\), and \\(\\text{CN}^-\\) is the base, accepting that proton to become \\(\\text{HCN}\\)."}]$op35$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex35$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is D because a Brønsted–Lowry acid is the proton donor and a Brønsted–Lowry base is the proton acceptor in a single proton-transfer event. Tracking the atoms across the arrow shows that \(\text{HCOOH}\) loses its acidic proton to become \(\text{HCOO}^-\); having donated a proton, \(\text{HCOOH}\) is therefore the acid. The \(\text{CN}^-\) ion gains that proton to become \(\text{HCN}\); having accepted a proton, \(\text{CN}^-\) is therefore the base. The accumulation of \(\text{HCOO}^-\) and \(\text{HCN}\) confirms this direction of transfer, with \(\text{HCOOH}/\text{HCOO}^-\) and \(\text{HCN}/\text{CN}^-\) forming the two conjugate pairs. (Choice A) This claims \(\text{HCOOH}\) is the base because oxygen is highly electronegative, but Brønsted–Lowry classification depends on whether a species donates or accepts a proton, not on the electronegativity of an atom it contains; \(\text{HCOOH}\) releases its proton and is the acid (misconception). (Choice B) This reverses the roles: it labels the proton acceptor \(\text{CN}^-\) as the donor and the proton donor \(\text{HCOOH}\) as the acceptor. The species that loses a proton is the acid and the species that gains one is the base, so this assignment inverts both definitions (reversed_relationship). (Choice C) This identifies \(\text{CN}^-\) as the acid by pointing to the product \(\text{HCN}\) that carries the proton, but the acid is defined by the act of donating a proton during the reaction, not by which species ends up holding the proton afterward; \(\text{CN}^-\) acquires the proton, so it is the base, not the acid (process_step_confusion). Skill 4$ex35$,
    'medium', '5A', $cc35$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc35$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a35A$Uses oxygen's electronegativity to assign the base role instead of proton-transfer behavior$a35A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a35B$Acid and base roles swapped (donor labeled acceptor and vice versa)$a35B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a35C$Calls the proton-gaining product's parent the acid by tracking where the proton lands rather than which species donated it$a35C$ FROM q;

-- Q4 [Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs · easy · Skill 1 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans C] (g36)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs',
    $qt36$Methylamine, \(\text{CH}_3\text{NH}_2\), is a weak base that reacts with water by accepting exactly one proton at its nitrogen atom. What is the conjugate acid formed when methylamine accepts this single proton?$qt36$,
    $op36$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{OH}^-\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{CH}_3\\text{NH}^-\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{CH}_3\\text{NH}_3^+\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{CH}_2\\text{NH}_2^-\\)"}]$op36$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex36$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. A Bronsted-Lowry base accepts a proton (\(\text{H}^+\)), and its conjugate acid is the species formed after that proton has been added. Adding one \(\text{H}^+\) increases the hydrogen count by one and the overall charge by one unit. The answer to this question is C because adding a single proton to neutral \(\text{CH}_3\text{NH}_2\) at the nitrogen gives \(\text{CH}_3\text{NH}_3^+\) (the methylammonium ion), which has one more hydrogen and a charge of \(+1\), exactly as required for the conjugate acid of a base. (Choice A) \(\text{OH}^-\) is the partner species generated from the water molecule that donates the proton (\(\text{CH}_3\text{NH}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{CH}_3\text{NH}_3^+ + \text{OH}^-\)); it is the conjugate base of water in this reaction, not the conjugate acid of methylamine. (Choice B) \(\text{CH}_3\text{NH}^-\) results from removing a proton from methylamine rather than adding one; that species is the conjugate base of methylamine, not its conjugate acid. (Choice D) \(\text{CH}_2\text{NH}_2^-\) is formed by stripping a hydrogen from the carbon atom, which is neither the site of proton acceptance nor an acid-base conjugate of methylamine. Skill 1$ex36$,
    'easy', '5A', $cc36$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc36$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a36A$Selected the water-derived partner species (OH-) rather than the protonated base itself$a36A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a36B$Lost a proton instead of gaining one (formed the conjugate base, not the conjugate acid)$a36B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a36D$Removed a hydrogen from carbon instead of adding a proton to nitrogen$a36D$ FROM q;

-- Q5 [Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs · medium · Skill 4 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans A] (g37)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs',
    $qt37$In a buffered solution the bicarbonate ion \(\text{HCO}_3^-\) acts as a Brønsted–Lowry acid by donating one proton to a surrounding water molecule. Which species is the conjugate base produced by this single proton donation?$qt37$,
    $op37$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{CO}_3^{2-}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{H}_2\\text{CO}_3\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{CO}_3^{-}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{OH}^-\\)"}]$op37$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex37$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is A because a Brønsted–Lowry conjugate base is what remains after an acid donates exactly one proton (\(\text{H}^+\)), and removing one \(\text{H}^+\) from \(\text{HCO}_3^-\) yields the carbonate ion \(\text{CO}_3^{2-}\). When \(\text{HCO}_3^-\) acts as the acid, the proton it donates is a bare \(\text{H}^+\): the hydrogen leaves without its electron, so the fragment retains that electron and the species becomes one unit more negative. Starting charge \(-1\) plus the \(-1\) left behind by the departed \(\text{H}^+\) gives a net charge of \(-2\), and the atom count drops by one hydrogen, so \(\text{HCO}_3^- \rightarrow \text{H}^+ + \text{CO}_3^{2-}\). The conjugate acid–base pair therefore differs by exactly one proton, \(\text{HCO}_3^-/\text{CO}_3^{2-}\). (Choice B) \(\text{H}_2\text{CO}_3\) is the conjugate acid of \(\text{HCO}_3^-\), formed when bicarbonate gains a proton while acting as a base; it is the reverse of the donation described, so it cannot be the conjugate base. (Choice C) \(\text{CO}_3^{-}\) results from removing the hydrogen as a neutral H atom (carrying its electron away) rather than as \(\text{H}^+\), which leaves the charge unchanged at \(-1\); a true proton donation removes only \(\text{H}^+\), so the charge must increase in magnitude to \(-2\). (Choice D) \(\text{OH}^-\) is not generated by deprotonating \(\text{HCO}_3^-\); it would arise from cleaving the ion into other fragments, whereas a conjugate base must be the original species minus one proton, preserving the carbon and the remaining oxygens. Skill 4$ex37$,
    'medium', '5A', $cc37$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc37$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
