-- Q8 [Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs · medium · Skill 1 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans D] (g40)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs',
    $qt40$A chemist must sort the species \(\text{Ag}^+\), \(\text{NH}_3\), and \(\text{HCl}\) by the broadest acid-base definition under which each one can act, given the constraint that Arrhenius behavior counts only in aqueous solution. Which species is classified as a Lewis acid yet qualifies as neither a Brønsted-Lowry acid nor an Arrhenius acid?$qt40$,
    $op40$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{HCl}\\), because it is the only species that releases \\(\\text{H}^+\\) in water"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{NH}_3\\), because its lone pair lets it accept a proton from solution"},{"label":"C","text":"All three, because every acid-base species is ultimately a Lewis acid"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{Ag}^+\\), because it accepts an electron pair without donating or releasing any \\(\\text{H}^+\\)"}]$op40$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex40$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is D because the three acid-base definitions are nested categories: every Arrhenius acid is also a Brønsted-Lowry acid, and every Brønsted-Lowry acid is also a Lewis acid, so the Lewis definition is the broadest and includes species that the narrower definitions exclude. An Arrhenius acid must release \(\text{H}^+\) in aqueous solution, and a Brønsted-Lowry acid must donate a proton (\(\text{H}^+\)) to another species; both definitions therefore require the species to contain and give up a proton. The Lewis definition only requires accepting an electron pair, which does not involve a proton at all. The bare cation \(\text{Ag}^+\) has empty orbitals and accepts a lone pair (for example from \(\text{NH}_3\) to form \([\text{Ag}(\text{NH}_3)_2]^+\)), so it is a Lewis acid; but because it has no proton to release or donate, it is neither an Arrhenius acid nor a Brønsted-Lowry acid. \(\text{Ag}^+\) is the species that sits only in the broadest (outermost) category. (Choice A) \(\text{HCl}\) does release \(\text{H}^+\) in water, so it is an Arrhenius acid and therefore also a Brønsted-Lowry acid and a Lewis acid; it qualifies under all three definitions, not under Lewis alone, so it fails the constraint. (Choice B) \(\text{NH}_3\) accepts a proton, which makes it a Brønsted-Lowry and Lewis base, not an acid of any kind, so it does not fit the requested classification. (Choice C) While it is true that every acid is a Lewis acid, this choice ignores the constraint that the target species must be excluded from the two narrower acid definitions; \(\text{HCl}\) is an Arrhenius and Brønsted-Lowry acid and \(\text{NH}_3\) is not an acid at all, so "all three" is not defensible. Skill 1$ex40$,
    'medium', '5A', $cc40$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc40$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
