-- Q9 [Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs · medium · Skill 4 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans C] (g41)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs',
    $qt41$When a bare proton combines with water to form hydronium, \(\text{H}^+ + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow \text{H}_3\text{O}^+\), the new \(\text{O}\!-\!\text{H}\) linkage is a coordinate covalent bond in which both shared electrons originate from the oxygen. Classifying this single act of bond formation under the Lewis model, which species is the Lewis acid and why?$qt41$,
    $op41$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\), because it donates the proton that initiates the reaction"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+\\), because the positive charge resides on it after the bond forms"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{H}^+\\), because it accepts the lone pair that oxygen supplies to form the bond"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\), because it accepts the proton into its lone pair"}]$op41$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex41$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is C because the Lewis model defines an acid as the electron-pair acceptor and a base as the electron-pair donor, and in this coordinate covalent bond the proton is the species that accepts the pair. The bare proton \(\text{H}^+\) has no electrons of its own and an empty \(1s\) orbital; oxygen in \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) carries lone pairs and donates one of them into that empty orbital, so \(\text{H}^+\) is the electron-pair acceptor (\(\text{Lewis acid}\)) and \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) is the electron-pair donor (\(\text{Lewis base}\)). The newly formed bond is described as coordinate covalent precisely because both shared electrons came from a single atom, the oxygen of water. (Choice A) misreads the Lewis criterion by using proton transfer rather than electron-pair direction; in the Lewis framework the relevant arrow is the electron pair, which moves from oxygen toward the proton, and water is the donor, not the species that makes \(\text{H}^+\) an acid. (Choice B) attributes the acid label to formal charge on the product, but \(\text{H}_3\text{O}^+\) is the adduct formed by the acid-base combination, and Lewis classification is assigned to the reactant that accepts the pair, not to whichever product bears a positive charge. (Choice D) correctly notes that water uses its lone pair but then mislabels water as the acceptor; water donates the lone pair, which makes it the Lewis base, while the proton is the acceptor and therefore the Lewis acid. Skill 4$ex41$,
    'medium', '5A', $cc41$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc41$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
