-- Q10 [Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs · medium · Skill 4 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans A] (g42)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs',
    $qt42$Dissolved in water, the bare \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) cation binds six water molecules to form \([\text{Fe}(\text{H}_2\text{O})_6]^{3+}\), yet the \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) center holds no ionizable protons of its own. Which classification correctly captures the role the \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) center plays as those six water molecules coordinate to it?$qt42$,
    $op42$[{"label":"A","text":"A Lewis acid, because the \\(\\text{Fe}^{3+}\\) center accepts a lone pair from each coordinating water oxygen to form coordinate covalent bonds."},{"label":"B","text":"A Lewis base, because the \\(\\text{Fe}^{3+}\\) center donates a pair of electrons to each coordinating water molecule."},{"label":"C","text":"A Brønsted-Lowry acid, because the bare \\(\\text{Fe}^{3+}\\) cation transfers a proton to each coordinating water molecule."},{"label":"D","text":"A Lewis acid, because the \\(\\text{Fe}^{3+}\\) center releases a proton to the surrounding water once the six bonds have formed."}]$op42$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex42$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is A because a Lewis acid is defined as an electron-pair acceptor, and the \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) center has empty valence orbitals and a high positive charge that let it accept a lone pair from the oxygen of each coordinating water, forming six coordinate covalent (dative) bonds. In this coordination step the water oxygens are the electron-pair donors (Lewis bases) and \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) is the electron-pair acceptor (Lewis acid); no proton is involved at this stage, which is why the metal center can be a Lewis acid even though the bare cation carries no protons of its own. Only after the complex \([\text{Fe}(\text{H}_2\text{O})_6]^{3+}\) is assembled do the now-polarized bound water molecules go on to donate protons, which is a separate Brønsted-Lowry step attributable to the bound water, not to the bare metal center. (Choice B) This reverses the donor and acceptor roles: \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) accepts the electron pairs rather than donating them, so it is the Lewis acid, while the water oxygens are the Lewis bases that donate. (Choice C) The bare \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) cation has no protons to transfer, so it cannot itself be a Brønsted-Lowry acid in the coordination step; the proton donation that follows comes from the bound water molecules, not from the metal center. (Choice D) The Lewis-acid label is assigned to the wrong step: \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) earns it by accepting electron pairs as the bonds form, not by releasing a proton, and the bare metal center has no proton to release in any case. Skill 4$ex42$,
    'medium', '5A', $cc42$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc42$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
