-- Q11 [Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs · hard · Skill 4 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans B] (g43)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs',
    $qt43$In aqueous solution, \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) reacts with six cyanide ions to form the hexacyanoferrate(III) complex \([\text{Fe(CN)}_6]^{3-}\), in which each carbon end of \(\text{CN}^-\) is bonded to the central iron and both bonding electrons of every \(\text{Fe–C}\) linkage originate from the cyanide. On the Lewis definition, which species acts as the Lewis base in this reaction, and why?$qt43$,
    $op43$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{Fe}^{3+}\\), because as the positively charged center it pulls electron density toward itself and donates that density into the coordinate covalent bonds."},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{CN}^-\\), because it donates a lone pair of electrons to the \\(\\text{Fe}^{3+}\\) center, forming a coordinate covalent bond."},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{Fe}^{3+}\\), because it accepts the lone pair of electrons supplied by each cyanide ion."},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{CN}^-\\), because it donates a proton (\\(\\text{H}^+\\)) to the iron center, which makes it the Brønsted–Lowry base in the complex."}]$op43$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex43$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is B because a Lewis base is defined as an electron-pair donor, and the stem states that both bonding electrons of every \(\text{Fe–C}\) linkage originate from the cyanide ion. Each \(\text{CN}^-\) carries a lone pair on its carbon atom and supplies that pair to the empty orbitals of the \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) center, forming a coordinate (dative) covalent bond in which one partner provides both shared electrons. The species furnishing the electron pair is the Lewis base, so \(\text{CN}^-\) is the Lewis base; the electron-pair-accepting \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) is correspondingly the Lewis acid. (Choice A) This misstates the direction of electron flow: \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) does not donate electron density into the bonds — its valence orbitals are empty, and it instead accepts the lone pairs from cyanide, making it the Lewis acid rather than the base. (Choice C) The reason given describes \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) accurately as the electron-pair acceptor, but accepting a lone pair is the definition of a Lewis acid, not a Lewis base, so identifying \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) as the base reverses the acid–base roles. (Choice D) Although \(\text{CN}^-\) is correctly named, the justification invokes the Brønsted–Lowry framework: no proton is transferred in forming \([\text{Fe(CN)}_6]^{3-}\), so the species is acting as a Lewis base by donating an electron pair, not by donating an \(\text{H}^+\). Skill 4$ex43$,
    'hard', '5A', $cc43$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc43$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
