-- Q12 [Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs · easy · Skill 4 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans D] (g44)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs',
    $qt44$In the reaction \(\text{NH}_3 + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{NH}_4^+ + \text{OH}^-\), water reacts with ammonia, whereas in \(\text{HCl} + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow \text{H}_3\text{O}^+ + \text{Cl}^-\) water reacts with hydrogen chloride. Identify the Bronsted role water plays in each reaction and the conjugate species it becomes.$qt44$,
    $op44$[{"label":"A","text":"With \\(\\text{NH}_3\\) water acts as a base and becomes \\(\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+\\); with \\(\\text{HCl}\\) water acts as an acid and becomes \\(\\text{OH}^-\\)."},{"label":"B","text":"In both reactions water acts as a Bronsted acid, donating \\(\\text{H}^+\\) to become \\(\\text{OH}^-\\)."},{"label":"C","text":"With \\(\\text{NH}_3\\) water acts as an acid and becomes \\(\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+\\); with \\(\\text{HCl}\\) water acts as a base and becomes \\(\\text{OH}^-\\)."},{"label":"D","text":"With \\(\\text{NH}_3\\) water acts as an acid, donating \\(\\text{H}^+\\) to become \\(\\text{OH}^-\\); with \\(\\text{HCl}\\) water acts as a base, accepting \\(\\text{H}^+\\) to become \\(\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+\\)."}]$op44$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex44$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is D because water is amphoteric (amphiprotic): it can donate a proton to act as a Bronsted acid or accept a proton to act as a Bronsted base, depending on the other reactant. In \(\text{NH}_3 + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{NH}_4^+ + \text{OH}^-\), ammonia takes a proton, so water is the proton donor (the Bronsted acid); losing one \(\text{H}^+\) leaves \(\text{OH}^-\), which is therefore water's conjugate base. In \(\text{HCl} + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow \text{H}_3\text{O}^+ + \text{Cl}^-\), \(\text{HCl}\) donates a proton, so water is the proton acceptor (the Bronsted base); gaining one \(\text{H}^+\) gives \(\text{H}_3\text{O}^+\), which is water's conjugate acid. A conjugate base always has one fewer proton than its acid, and a conjugate acid always has one more proton than its base, so an acidic water becomes \(\text{OH}^-\) and a basic water becomes \(\text{H}_3\text{O}^+\). (Choice A) The roles are correctly split between the two reactions but the conjugate species are swapped: an acid donates \(\text{H}^+\) and must become \(\text{OH}^-\) (not \(\text{H}_3\text{O}^+\)), and a base accepts \(\text{H}^+\) and must become \(\text{H}_3\text{O}^+\) (not \(\text{OH}^-\)). (Choice B) Water does not act as an acid in both reactions; with \(\text{HCl}\) the proton flows from \(\text{HCl}\) to water, so water accepts a proton and acts as a base, producing \(\text{H}_3\text{O}^+\) rather than \(\text{OH}^-\). (Choice C) The acid/base labels are correct (acid with \(\text{NH}_3\), base with \(\text{HCl}\)) but the conjugate species are interchanged: donating a proton to \(\text{NH}_3\) yields \(\text{OH}^-\), and accepting a proton from \(\text{HCl}\) yields \(\text{H}_3\text{O}^+\). Skill 4$ex44$,
    'easy', '5A', $cc44$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc44$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
