UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a44C$Assigns the correct acid/base role in each reaction but confuses the proton-transfer step, mismatching the conjugate species (OH- vs H3O+) to the roles$a44C$ FROM q;

-- Q13 [Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs · hard · Skill 4 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans D] (g45)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs',
    $qt45$The bisulfite ion \(\text{HSO}_3^-\) is amphiprotic, so it can function as a Bronsted-Lowry acid in one proton transfer and as a Bronsted-Lowry base in another. Which option correctly pairs a reaction in which \(\text{HSO}_3^-\) acts as an acid (donating a proton) with a reaction in which the same ion acts as a base (accepting a proton), with the products in each case being the species produced by that single proton transfer?$qt45$,
    $op45$[{"label":"A","text":"Acid: \\(\\text{HSO}_3^- + \\text{OH}^- \\rightleftharpoons \\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_3 + \\text{OH}^-\\); Base: \\(\\text{HSO}_3^- + \\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+ \\rightleftharpoons \\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_3 + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"Acid: \\(\\text{HSO}_3^- + \\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+ \\rightleftharpoons \\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_3 + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\); Base: \\(\\text{HSO}_3^- + \\text{OH}^- \\rightleftharpoons \\text{SO}_3^{2-} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"Acid: \\(\\text{HSO}_3^- + \\text{OH}^- \\rightleftharpoons \\text{SO}_3^{2-} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\); Base: \\(\\text{HSO}_3^- + \\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\rightleftharpoons \\text{O}^{2-} + \\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_3\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"Acid: \\(\\text{HSO}_3^- + \\text{OH}^- \\rightleftharpoons \\text{SO}_3^{2-} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\); Base: \\(\\text{HSO}_3^- + \\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+ \\rightleftharpoons \\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_3 + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\)"}]$op45$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex45$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is D because an amphiprotic species can either donate or accept a single proton, and each role must conserve both mass and charge. When \(\text{HSO}_3^-\) acts as a Bronsted-Lowry acid, it donates one proton to a base such as \(\text{OH}^-\); losing \(\text{H}^+\) from \(\text{HSO}_3^-\) leaves \(\text{SO}_3^{2-}\) (sulfite), and the abstracted proton joins \(\text{OH}^-\) to form \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\), giving \(\text{HSO}_3^- + \text{OH}^- \rightleftharpoons \text{SO}_3^{2-} + \text{H}_2\text{O}\) (charge: \(-1 + -1 = -2 + 0\), balanced). When the same ion acts as a Bronsted-Lowry base, it accepts one proton from an acid such as \(\text{H}_3\text{O}^+\); gaining \(\text{H}^+\) converts \(\text{HSO}_3^-\) into \(\text{H}_2\text{SO}_3\) (sulfurous acid), and \(\text{H}_3\text{O}^+\) that donated the proton becomes \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\), giving \(\text{HSO}_3^- + \text{H}_3\text{O}^+ \rightleftharpoons \text{H}_2\text{SO}_3 + \text{H}_2\text{O}\) (charge: \(-1 + +1 = 0 + 0\), balanced). Only option D assigns the correct product to each role. (Choice A) contains an acid reaction that is mass- and charge-inconsistent: writing \(\text{HSO}_3^- + \text{OH}^- \rightleftharpoons \text{H}_2\text{SO}_3 + \text{OH}^-\) adds a proton to \(\text{HSO}_3^-\) while leaving \(\text{OH}^-\) unchanged, which conserves neither hydrogen count nor charge and confuses the proton-donation step with proton gain. (Choice B) reverses the two roles: the reaction with \(\text{H}_3\text{O}^+\) (in which \(\text{HSO}_3^-\) gains a proton to become \(\text{H}_2\text{SO}_3\)) is proton acceptance, which is the base role, not the acid role; likewise the reaction with \(\text{OH}^-\) producing \(\text{SO}_3^{2-}\) is proton donation, the acid role, so the acid and base labels are swapped. (Choice C) keeps the correct acid reaction but invents an impossible product for the base step: an oxide ion \(\text{O}^{2-}\) does not arise from \(\text{HSO}_3^-\) accepting a proton, and the written equation conserves neither hydrogen count nor charge (the left side carries a net charge of \(-1\) while the right side carries \(-2\)), so it cannot represent any Bronsted-Lowry proton transfer. Skill 4$ex45$,
    'hard', '5A', $cc45$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc45$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
