-- Q14 [Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs · hard · Skill 2 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans A] (g46)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs',
    $qt46$Lactic acid (\(\text{HC}_3\text{H}_5\text{O}_3\)) is a weak monoprotic acid with \(K_a = 1.4 \times 10^{-3}\) at 25 °C. For its conjugate base, the lactate ion (\(\text{C}_3\text{H}_5\text{O}_3^-\)), what is \(K_b\)?$qt46$,
    $op46$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(7.1 \\times 10^{-12}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(7.1 \\times 10^{-17}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(1.4 \\times 10^{-17}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(7.1 \\times 10^{11}\\)"}]$op46$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex46$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is A because for any conjugate acid-base pair at 25 °C the ion-product of water links the two ionization constants by \(K_a \times K_b = K_w = 1.0 \times 10^{-14}\), so \(K_b\) of the conjugate base is found by dividing: \(K_b = K_w / K_a = (1.0 \times 10^{-14}) / (1.4 \times 10^{-3})\). Dividing the mantissas gives \(1.0 / 1.4 = 0.71\), and subtracting the exponents gives \(10^{-14 - (-3)} = 10^{-11}\), so \(K_b = 0.71 \times 10^{-11} = 7.1 \times 10^{-12}\). This small \(K_b\) reflects the inverse-strength relationship within a conjugate pair: lactic acid is a comparatively strong weak acid (\(K_a\) on the order of \(10^{-3}\)), so its conjugate base lactate is correspondingly very weak (\(K_b\) on the order of \(10^{-12}\)). (Choice B) \(7.1 \times 10^{-17}\) keeps the correct mantissa \(0.71\) but adds the exponents \((-14) + (-3) = -17\) instead of subtracting them; dividing powers of ten requires subtracting the divisor's exponent, which yields \(-11\), not \(-17\). (Choice C) \(1.4 \times 10^{-17}\) comes from multiplying \(K_a \times K_w\) rather than dividing, treating the relationship as \(K_b = K_a \cdot K_w\); the defining relation is \(K_a \cdot K_b = K_w\), so \(K_b\) must be obtained by division. (Choice D) \(7.1 \times 10^{11}\) uses the correct mantissa but inverts the sign of \(K_w\)'s exponent, computing \(10^{+14 - 3}\); because \(K_w = 1.0 \times 10^{-14}\) the exponent is negative, giving \(10^{-11}\) and a tiny (not enormous) \(K_b\). Skill 2$ex46$,
    'hard', '5A', $cc46$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc46$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
