-- Q15 [Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions · easy · Skill 1 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans A] (g47)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions',
    $qt47$Solid \(\text{NaCl}\), the salt produced by neutralizing \(\text{HCl}\) with \(\text{NaOH}\), is dissolved in pure water at \(25^\circ\text{C}\) to give a \(0.10\ \text{M}\) solution. The resulting solution is neutral because$qt47$,
    $op47$[{"label":"A","text":"neither \\(\\text{Na}^+\\) nor \\(\\text{Cl}^-\\) hydrolyzes appreciably, since they come from a strong base and a strong acid, so neither shifts \\([\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+]\\)."},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{Cl}^-\\) hydrolyzes to form \\(\\text{HCl}\\) while \\(\\text{Na}^+\\) hydrolyzes to form \\(\\text{NaOH}\\), and these two effects cancel to leave the \\(\\text{pH}\\) at \\(7\\)."},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{Cl}^-\\) is the conjugate base of the strong acid \\(\\text{HCl}\\), so it accepts a proton from water and is balanced by an equal release of \\(\\text{OH}^-\\)."},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{Na}^+\\) is a strong Lewis acid that consumes \\(\\text{OH}^-\\), an effect that is exactly offset by \\(\\text{Cl}^-\\) consuming \\(\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+\\)."}]$op47$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex47$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is A because \(\text{NaCl}\) is the salt of a strong base (\(\text{NaOH}\)) and a strong acid (\(\text{HCl}\)), so both of its ions are spectator ions that do not react measurably with water. \(\text{Na}^+\) is the cation of a strong base, so it has no tendency to accept or donate protons; \(\text{Cl}^-\) is the conjugate base of the strong acid \(\text{HCl}\), and conjugates of strong acids are such weak bases that their reaction with water is negligible. With neither ion altering \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\) or \([\text{OH}^-]\), the autoionization of water alone sets \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+] = [\text{OH}^-]\) and \(\text{pH} = 7\) at \(25^\circ\text{C}\). (Choice B) This reverses the actual behavior: neither \(\text{Cl}^-\) nor \(\text{Na}^+\) hydrolyzes to regenerate the parent acid and base, so there are no opposing reactions to cancel; the solution is neutral because no hydrolysis occurs, not because two hydrolysis reactions offset. (Choice C) \(\text{Cl}^-\) is indeed the conjugate base of \(\text{HCl}\), but because \(\text{HCl}\) is a strong acid its conjugate base is exceedingly weak and does not accept a proton from water appreciably; it does not generate \(\text{OH}^-\). (Choice D) \(\text{Na}^+\) is not an acid that consumes \(\text{OH}^-\); the cation of a strong base is a spectator, so there is no acid-base effect to be offset, and the premise of two canceling reactions is incorrect. Skill 1$ex47$,
    'easy', '5A', $cc47$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc47$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
