-- Q16 [Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions · easy · Skill 4 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans B] (g48)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions',
    $qt48$Solid ammonium chloride, \(\text{NH}_4\text{Cl}\) — the salt of the strong acid HCl and the weak base \(\text{NH}_3\) — is dissolved in pure water to give a \(0.10\,\text{M}\) solution. Predict whether the resulting solution is acidic, basic, or neutral, and identify the species responsible.$qt48$,
    $op48$[{"label":"A","text":"Basic, because \\(\\text{NH}_4^+\\) accepts a proton from water to release \\(\\text{OH}^-\\)."},{"label":"B","text":"Acidic, because \\(\\text{NH}_4^+\\) is a weak acid that donates a proton to water while \\(\\text{Cl}^-\\) is a spectator."},{"label":"C","text":"Neutral, because both \\(\\text{NH}_4^+\\) and \\(\\text{Cl}^-\\) are spectator ions that do not react with water."},{"label":"D","text":"Acidic, because \\(\\text{Cl}^-\\) hydrolyzes to donate a proton to water while \\(\\text{NH}_4^+\\) is a spectator."}]$op48$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex48$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is B because the cation of a salt comes from the base and the anion comes from the acid, and only an ion derived from a weak parent will hydrolyze. \(\text{NH}_4\text{Cl}\) dissolves completely into \(\text{NH}_4^+\) and \(\text{Cl}^-\). The cation \(\text{NH}_4^+\) is the conjugate acid of the weak base \(\text{NH}_3\), so it has a measurable \(K_a > 0\) and acts as a weak acid, donating a proton to water: \(\text{NH}_4^+ + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{NH}_3 + \text{H}_3\text{O}^+\). The anion \(\text{Cl}^-\) is the conjugate base of the strong acid HCl; because HCl is essentially fully ionized, \(\text{Cl}^-\) is an exceedingly weak base that does not hydrolyze and is an inert spectator. With only \(\text{NH}_4^+\) reacting, the solution accumulates \(\text{H}_3\text{O}^+\), so \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+] > [\text{OH}^-]\) and the \(\text{pH}\) falls below 7: a salt of a strong acid and a weak base is acidic. (Choice A) This reverses the role of \(\text{NH}_4^+\): an acidic cation donates a proton, it does not accept one, so it cannot generate \(\text{OH}^-\); accepting a proton to make base describes the parent \(\text{NH}_3\), not the ammonium ion. (Choice C) Treating both ions as inert spectators is the rule for a salt of a strong acid and a strong base (such as \(\text{NaCl}\)); here the cation derives from a weak base, so \(\text{NH}_4^+\) is not a spectator and the solution is not neutral. (Choice D) The acidic prediction is correct, but the responsible species is misassigned: \(\text{Cl}^-\) is the conjugate base of a strong acid and does not hydrolyze, whereas \(\text{NH}_4^+\) is the proton donor that lowers the \(\text{pH}\). Skill 4$ex48$,
    'easy', '5A', $cc48$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc48$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
