-- Q17 [Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions · easy · Skill 4 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans C] (g49)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions',
    $qt49$Solid sodium acetate \(\text{NaC}_2\text{H}_3\text{O}_2\), the salt of acetic acid (a weak acid) and \(\text{NaOH}\) (a strong base), is dissolved in pure water at \(25\,^{\circ}\text{C}\) to make a \(0.10\ \text{M}\) solution. Which prediction about the resulting solution is best supported by the ions it produces?$qt49$,
    $op49$[{"label":"A","text":"The solution is acidic (\\(\\text{pH} < 7\\)) because \\(\\text{Na}^+\\) hydrolyzes water to release \\(\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+\\)."},{"label":"B","text":"The solution is neutral (\\(\\text{pH} = 7\\)) because both \\(\\text{Na}^+\\) and \\(\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_3\\text{O}_2^-\\) are spectator ions that do not react with water."},{"label":"C","text":"The solution is basic (\\(\\text{pH} > 7\\)) because \\(\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_3\\text{O}_2^-\\) is a weak base (\\(K_b > 0\\)) while \\(\\text{Na}^+\\) is an inert spectator."},{"label":"D","text":"The solution is basic (\\(\\text{pH} > 7\\)) because \\(\\text{Na}^+\\) is the conjugate base of the strong base \\(\\text{NaOH}\\) and reacts with water to release \\(\\text{OH}^-\\)."}]$op49$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex49$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is C because the pH of a salt solution is set by which dissolved ion, if any, exchanges a proton with water, and for sodium acetate only the acetate anion does so. When \(\text{NaC}_2\text{H}_3\text{O}_2\) dissolves it dissociates completely into \(\text{Na}^+\) and \(\text{C}_2\text{H}_3\text{O}_2^-\). Acetate is the conjugate base of the weak acid acetic acid; because acetic acid holds its proton appreciably, its conjugate base is strong enough to accept a proton from water, \(\text{C}_2\text{H}_3\text{O}_2^- + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{HC}_2\text{H}_3\text{O}_2 + \text{OH}^-\), a hydrolysis with \(K_b = K_w/K_a > 0\). This reaction generates excess \(\text{OH}^-\), so \([\text{OH}^-] > [\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\) and \(\text{pH} > 7\). The cation \(\text{Na}^+\) comes from the strong base \(\text{NaOH}\); cations of strong bases are inert spectators that do not hydrolyze, so they leave the pH unchanged. The general rule for a salt of a weak acid and a strong base is therefore a basic solution. (Choice A) \(\text{Na}^+\) is the cation of a strong base and does not react with water; it cannot generate \(\text{H}_3\text{O}^+\), so the solution is not acidic on this basis. (Choice B) Only salts of a strong acid and a strong base give neutral solutions; here acetate is not a spectator but a weak base that hydrolyzes, so the solution is not neutral. (Choice D) The conclusion that the solution is basic is correct, but the reasoning is reversed: \(\text{Na}^+\) is the spectator cation of \(\text{NaOH}\) and does not react with water, whereas it is the acetate anion that produces \(\text{OH}^-\). Skill 4$ex49$,
    'easy', '5A', $cc49$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc49$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
