-- Q18 [Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions · medium · Skill 4 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans D] (g50)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions',
    $qt50$Solid \(\text{KCN}\), the salt of the weak acid \(\text{HCN}\) (\(K_a = 4.9\times10^{-10}\)) and the strong base \(\text{KOH}\), is dissolved in pure water to make a \(0.10\ \text{M}\) solution. Which prediction about the resulting \(\text{pH}\), and the ion responsible for it, is correct?$qt50$,
    $op50$[{"label":"A","text":"Acidic, because \\(\\text{K}^+\\) is a small, highly charged cation that hydrolyzes water to release \\(\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+\\)."},{"label":"B","text":"Neutral (\\(\\text{pH} = 7\\)), because \\(\\text{KCN}\\) is the product of an acid reacting completely with a base, so its ions cannot shift the water equilibrium."},{"label":"C","text":"Basic, because the spectator ion \\(\\text{CN}^-\\) holds \\(\\text{K}^+\\) in solution while \\(\\text{K}^+\\) abstracts a proton from water to generate \\(\\text{OH}^-\\)."},{"label":"D","text":"Basic, because \\(\\text{CN}^-\\) is the conjugate base of a weak acid and hydrolyzes (\\(\\text{CN}^- + \\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\rightleftharpoons \\text{HCN} + \\text{OH}^-\\)), while \\(\\text{K}^+\\) is a spectator."}]$op50$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex50$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is D because the only ion in \(\text{KCN}\) that disturbs the water equilibrium is \(\text{CN}^-\), the conjugate base of the weak acid \(\text{HCN}\), so it hydrolyzes to give a basic solution. To classify a salt, split it into its parent ions and ask whether each came from a strong or weak partner. \(\text{K}^+\) is the conjugate acid of the strong base \(\text{KOH}\); a cation derived from a strong base is a spectator and does not affect \(\text{pH}\). \(\text{CN}^-\) is the conjugate base of the weak acid \(\text{HCN}\), and because \(\text{HCN}\) is weak, \(\text{CN}^-\) is appreciably basic. It reacts with water as \(\text{CN}^- + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{HCN} + \text{OH}^-\), with \(K_b = K_w/K_a = (1.0\times10^{-14})/(4.9\times10^{-10}) \approx 2.0\times10^{-5}\). Producing \(\text{OH}^-\) drives the solution basic (\(\text{pH} > 7\)). (Choice A) is a misconception: \(\text{K}^+\) is the conjugate of a strong base and is too large with too low a charge density to hydrolyze water; only small, highly charged cations (e.g. \(\text{Al}^{3+}\)) acidify a solution, and \(\text{K}^+\) is not one of them. (Choice B) is the "salt always equals neutral" fallacy; a neutralization can produce a salt whose ions do shift the water equilibrium, and here \(\text{CN}^-\) clearly does, so the solution is not \(\text{pH} = 7\). (Choice C) reverses the roles of the two ions: it correctly predicts a basic solution but credits the proton abstraction to \(\text{K}^+\) and labels \(\text{CN}^-\) the spectator, whereas it is \(\text{CN}^-\) that accepts the proton and \(\text{K}^+\) that is inert. Skill 4$ex50$,
    'medium', '5A', $cc50$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc50$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
