-- Q19 [Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions · medium · Skill 1 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans A] (g51)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions',
    $qt51$Solid sodium fluoride (NaF) is dissolved in pure water at 25 °C, given that \(K_a\) for HF is \(6.8\times10^{-4}\). Which ion drives the resulting solution's pH away from 7, and in which direction does the pH move?$qt51$,
    $op51$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{F}^-\\) hydrolyzes as a weak base while \\(\\text{Na}^+\\) is a spectator, so the pH rises above 7"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{Na}^+\\) hydrolyzes as a weak acid while \\(\\text{F}^-\\) is a spectator, so the pH falls below 7"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{F}^-\\) hydrolyzes as a weak acid while \\(\\text{Na}^+\\) is a spectator, so the pH falls below 7"},{"label":"D","text":"Both \\(\\text{Na}^+\\) and \\(\\text{F}^-\\) are spectator ions, so the pH stays at 7"}]$op51$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex51$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is A because \(\text{F}^-\) is the conjugate base of the weak acid HF and therefore hydrolyzes water, while \(\text{Na}^+\) is the conjugate of the strong base NaOH and does nothing. NaF dissociates completely into \(\text{Na}^+\) and \(\text{F}^-\). To decide whether an ion affects pH, trace it back to its parent acid or base. \(\text{Na}^+\) comes from NaOH, a strong base that is fully dissociated and has no tendency to re-form; an ion whose parent is a strong electrolyte does not hydrolyze, so \(\text{Na}^+\) is an inert spectator. \(\text{F}^-\) comes from HF, a weak acid (\(K_a = 6.8\times10^{-4}\)) that only partially ionizes; because HF holds onto protons appreciably, its conjugate base \(\text{F}^-\) is strong enough to pull a proton from water: \(\text{F}^- + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{HF} + \text{OH}^-\). This reaction generates \(\text{OH}^-\), so \([\text{OH}^-] > [\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\) and the \(\text{pH}\) rises above 7. Quantitatively, \(K_b\) for \(\text{F}^-\) is \(K_w/K_a = (1.0\times10^{-14})/(6.8\times10^{-4}) \approx 1.5\times10^{-11}\), small but nonzero, confirming a weakly basic solution. The general rule: a salt of a strong base and a weak acid gives a basic solution. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question.

(Choice B) This reverses both roles, claiming \(\text{Na}^+\) hydrolyzes as a weak acid and \(\text{F}^-\) is the spectator. In fact \(\text{Na}^+\) is the conjugate of the strong base NaOH and is the spectator; it cannot donate a proton. \(\text{F}^-\) is the species that reacts with water. The predicted acidic pH is also wrong because no proton-donating ion is present.

(Choice C) This correctly identifies \(\text{F}^-\) as the reactive ion and \(\text{Na}^+\) as the spectator, but mislabels \(\text{F}^-\) as a weak acid. \(\text{F}^-\) is the conjugate base of HF and acts as a weak base, accepting a proton to release \(\text{OH}^-\); it has no acidic proton to donate. Because it produces \(\text{OH}^-\), the pH rises above 7 rather than falling below it.

(Choice D) Treating both ions as spectators describes a salt of a strong acid and a strong base (for example, NaCl), which gives a neutral solution. NaF is not that case: although \(\text{Na}^+\) is indeed a spectator, \(\text{F}^-\) is the conjugate base of a weak acid and does hydrolyze, so the solution is not neutral. Skill 1$ex51$,
    'medium', '5A', $cc51$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc51$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
