-- Q20 [Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions · medium · Skill 4 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans B] (g52)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions',
    $qt52$Solid ammonium fluoride, \(\text{NH}_4\text{F}\), is dissolved in pure water at \(25\,^\circ\text{C}\) to give a \(0.10\,\text{M}\) solution. Unlike the chloride salt, both ions of this salt react with water: the cation has \(K_a(\text{NH}_4^+) = 5.6\times10^{-10}\) and the anion has \(K_b(\text{F}^-) = 1.5\times10^{-11}\). Predict whether the resulting solution is acidic, basic, or neutral, and identify the reason.$qt52$,
    $op52$[{"label":"A","text":"Basic, because \\(K_b(\\text{F}^-) > K_a(\\text{NH}_4^+)\\), so anion hydrolysis producing \\(\\text{OH}^-\\) outcompetes cation hydrolysis."},{"label":"B","text":"Acidic, because \\(K_a(\\text{NH}_4^+) > K_b(\\text{F}^-)\\), so cation hydrolysis producing \\(\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+\\) outcompetes anion hydrolysis."},{"label":"C","text":"Neutral, because both ions hydrolyze, so the \\(\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+\\) and \\(\\text{OH}^-\\) they generate cancel exactly."},{"label":"D","text":"Acidic, because \\(\\text{NH}_4^+\\) is a weak acid while \\(\\text{F}^-\\) is an inert spectator that does not react with water."}]$op52$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex52$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is B because \(\text{NH}_4\text{F}\) is the salt of a weak base (\(\text{NH}_3\)) and a weak acid (\(\text{HF}\)), so both of its ions react with water: the cation as a weak acid, \(\text{NH}_4^+ + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{NH}_3 + \text{H}_3\text{O}^+\) with \(K_a = 5.6\times10^{-10}\), and the anion as a weak base, \(\text{F}^- + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{HF} + \text{OH}^-\) with \(K_b = 1.5\times10^{-11}\). Because the two ions are present at equal concentration, the larger equilibrium constant determines the dominant process. Since \(K_a(\text{NH}_4^+) = 5.6\times10^{-10}\) exceeds \(K_b(\text{F}^-) = 1.5\times10^{-11}\) by a factor of roughly \(38\), cation hydrolysis generates more \(\text{H}_3\text{O}^+\) than anion hydrolysis generates \(\text{OH}^-\), leaving a net excess of \(\text{H}_3\text{O}^+\) and a pH below \(7\). (Choice A) reverses the comparison: it asserts that the anion's constant is larger, but \(K_b(\text{F}^-)\) is actually the smaller value, so anion hydrolysis is the weaker process and cannot dominate. (Choice C) incorrectly assumes the two hydrolysis reactions offset exactly; cancellation would require \(K_a = K_b\), but the constants here differ by more than an order of magnitude, so the products do not balance and the solution cannot be neutral. (Choice D) reaches the right classification by faulty reasoning: it treats \(\text{F}^-\) as an inert spectator like \(\text{Cl}^-\), but \(\text{F}^-\) is the conjugate base of the weak acid \(\text{HF}\) and does hydrolyze; the solution is acidic because \(K_a > K_b\), not because the anion is unreactive. Skill 4$ex52$,
    'medium', '5A', $cc52$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc52$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
