-- Q21 [Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions · medium · Skill 2 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans C] (g53)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions',
    $qt53$Sodium acetate dissolves to release acetate, the conjugate base of acetic acid \((K_a = 1.8 \times 10^{-5})\). Before computing the solution's pH, a student must find \(K_b\) for acetate using \(K_a \times K_b = K_w = 1.0 \times 10^{-14}\) at 25 °C. What is \(K_b\) of the acetate ion?$qt53$,
    $op53$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(1.8 \\times 10^{-19}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(5.6 \\times 10^{-3}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(5.6 \\times 10^{-10}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(1.8 \\times 10^{9}\\)"}]$op53$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex53$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is C because the conjugate base \(K_b\) is obtained by dividing \(K_w\) by the parent acid's \(K_a\). For any conjugate acid–base pair, \(K_a \times K_b = K_w\), so \(K_b = \dfrac{K_w}{K_a} = \dfrac{1.0 \times 10^{-14}}{1.8 \times 10^{-5}}\). Dividing the coefficients gives \(1.0 / 1.8 = 0.56\), and subtracting exponents gives \(10^{-14 - (-5)} = 10^{-9}\), so \(K_b = 0.56 \times 10^{-9} = 5.6 \times 10^{-10}\). This small \(K_b\) correctly reflects that acetate is only a weak base. (Choice A) \(1.8 \times 10^{-19}\) comes from multiplying \(K_a\) by \(K_w\) \((1.8 \times 10^{-5})(1.0 \times 10^{-14})\) instead of dividing; the relationship \(K_a \times K_b = K_w\) must be solved for \(K_b\) by division, not by multiplying the two known constants together. (Choice B) \(5.6 \times 10^{-3}\) results from using \(1.0 \times 10^{-7}\) for \(K_w\); \(1.0 \times 10^{-7}\) is the neutral hydronium concentration \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\) at 25 °C, whereas \(K_w = [\text{H}_3\text{O}^+][\text{OH}^-] = 1.0 \times 10^{-14}\). (Choice D) \(1.8 \times 10^{9}\) inverts the division, computing \(K_a / K_w\) rather than \(K_w / K_a\); this would imply acetate is an extremely strong base, contradicting the rule that the conjugate of a weak acid is itself weak. Skill 2$ex53$,
    'medium', '5A', $cc53$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc53$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
