-- Q22 [Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions · medium · Skill 2 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans D] (g54)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions',
    $qt54$A \(0.10\ \text{M}\) solution of sodium acetate, \(\text{CH}_3\text{COONa}\), is prepared in water; the acetate ion is the conjugate base of acetic acid, for which \(K_a = 1.8\times10^{-5}\) (with \(K_w = 1.0\times10^{-14}\)). Treating dissociation of the dissolved salt as complete and the resulting \(0.10\ \text{M}\ \text{CH}_3\text{COO}^-\) as the only species that reacts with water, what is the \(\text{pH}\) of the solution?$qt54$,
    $op54$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 7.00\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 2.87\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 5.13\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 8.87\\)"}]$op54$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex54$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is D because the acetate ion is the conjugate base of a weak acid paired with the spectator cation \(\text{Na}^+\), so it hydrolyzes water to make the solution basic, and the \(\text{pH}\) works out to \(8.87\). The governing equilibrium is \(\text{CH}_3\text{COO}^- + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{CH}_3\text{COOH} + \text{OH}^-\), described by \(K_b = K_w/K_a = (1.0\times10^{-14})/(1.8\times10^{-5}) = 5.6\times10^{-10}\). Because \(K_b\) is tiny, the small-\(x\) approximation gives \([\text{OH}^-] = \sqrt{K_b\,C} = \sqrt{(5.6\times10^{-10})(0.10)} = \sqrt{5.6\times10^{-11}} = 7.5\times10^{-6}\ \text{M}\). Then \(\text{pOH} = -\log(7.5\times10^{-6}) = 5.13\), and \(\text{pH} = 14.00 - \text{pOH} = 14.00 - 5.13 = 8.87\), correctly basic. (Choice A) \(\text{pH} = 7.00\) treats the salt as neutral, as if it came from a strong acid and strong base; sodium acetate derives from a weak acid (acetic) and a strong base, so its conjugate-base anion hydrolyzes and the solution is basic, not neutral. (Choice B) \(\text{pH} = 2.87\) comes from treating acetate as a weak acid and using \([\text{H}^+] = \sqrt{K_a\,C} = \sqrt{(1.8\times10^{-5})(0.10)} = 1.3\times10^{-3}\ \text{M}\); acetate is a base, so \(K_b = K_w/K_a\) must be used and the result must be basic, not acidic. (Choice C) \(\text{pH} = 5.13\) is the \(\text{pOH}\) reported as the \(\text{pH}\); the \([\text{OH}^-]\) was found correctly, but the final step \(\text{pH} = 14.00 - \text{pOH}\) was skipped, leaving an acidic value for what is in fact a basic solution. Skill 2$ex54$,
    'medium', '5A', $cc54$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc54$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
