UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a54B$conjugate base treated as a weak acid using Ka instead of Kb$a54B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a54C$pOH reported as pH, skipping pH = 14 - pOH$a54C$ FROM q;

-- Q23 [Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions · medium · Skill 2 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans A] (g55)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions',
    $qt55$A \(0.10\ \text{M}\) solution of ammonium chloride, \(\text{NH}_4\text{Cl}\), is prepared at \(25\,^\circ\text{C}\), where \(K_w = 1.0\times10^{-14}\) and the base ionization constant of ammonia is \(K_b = 1.8\times10^{-5}\). Treating the dissolved salt as the only solute, what is the \(\text{pH}\) of this solution?$qt55$,
    $op55$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 5.13\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 11.13\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 10.25\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 2.87\\)"}]$op55$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex55$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is A because \(\text{NH}_4\text{Cl}\) is the salt of a strong acid (\(\text{HCl}\)) and a weak base (\(\text{NH}_3\)): the chloride ion is the conjugate base of a strong acid and does not hydrolyze, while the ammonium ion \(\text{NH}_4^+\) is the conjugate acid of the weak base ammonia and donates a proton to water, making the solution acidic. First convert the supplied \(K_b\) of ammonia into the acid ionization constant of \(\text{NH}_4^+\): \(K_a = \dfrac{K_w}{K_b} = \dfrac{1.0\times10^{-14}}{1.8\times10^{-5}} = 5.6\times10^{-10}\). For the equilibrium \(\text{NH}_4^+ + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{NH}_3 + \text{H}_3\text{O}^+\), the small \(K_a\) justifies the approximation \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+] = \sqrt{K_a\,C} = \sqrt{(5.6\times10^{-10})(0.10)} = \sqrt{5.6\times10^{-11}} = 7.5\times10^{-6}\ \text{M}\). Then \(\text{pH} = -\log(7.5\times10^{-6}) = 5.13\), confirming a mildly acidic solution. (Choice B) \(\text{pH} = 11.13\) treats the salt as basic by applying \(K_b = 1.8\times10^{-5}\) directly to the salt to find \([\text{OH}^-] = \sqrt{K_b\,C} = 1.3\times10^{-3}\ \text{M}\) and then converting \(\text{pOH} = 2.87\) to \(\text{pH}\); this reverses the chemistry, since the ammonium ion acts as an acid, not a base, and the solution cannot be basic. (Choice C) \(\text{pH} = 10.25\) omits the square root and sets \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+] = K_a\,C = 5.6\times10^{-11}\ \text{M}\), so \(-\log(5.6\times10^{-11}) = 10.25\); the equilibrium expression \(K_a = x^2/C\) requires solving for \(x = \sqrt{K_a\,C}\) rather than equating \(x\) with the product \(K_a\,C\). (Choice D) \(\text{pH} = 2.87\) skips the \(K_a = K_w/K_b\) conversion and uses the ammonia \(K_b = 1.8\times10^{-5}\) itself as the acid ionization constant, giving \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+] = \sqrt{(1.8\times10^{-5})(0.10)} = 1.3\times10^{-3}\ \text{M}\) and \(\text{pH} = 2.87\); using the base constant in place of the much smaller derived \(K_a\) overstates the acidity by several orders of magnitude. Skill 2$ex55$,
    'medium', '5A', $cc55$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc55$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
