-- Q25 [Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions · hard · Skill 4 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans C] (g57)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions',
    $qt57$Two salts are dissolved separately at the same concentration, \(0.10\ \text{M}\): sodium hypochlorite (\(\text{NaOCl}\), whose parent acid \(\text{HClO}\) has \(K_a = 2.9\times10^{-8}\)) and sodium nitrite (\(\text{NaNO}_2\), whose parent acid \(\text{HNO}_2\) has \(K_a = 4.6\times10^{-4}\)). In each case the cation is the spectator \(\text{Na}^+\) from a strong base and only the anion undergoes hydrolysis. Which solution is the more basic (higher \(\text{pH}\)), and why?$qt57$,
    $op57$[{"label":"A","text":"The \\(\\text{NaNO}_2\\) solution, because its larger parent-acid \\(K_a\\) means more of the \\(\\text{NO}_2^-\\) anion is available to react with water and generate \\(\\text{OH}^-\\)."},{"label":"B","text":"The \\(\\text{NaNO}_2\\) solution, because the stronger parent acid \\(\\text{HNO}_2\\) furnishes the stronger conjugate base \\(\\text{NO}_2^-\\), giving it the larger \\(K_b\\)."},{"label":"C","text":"The \\(\\text{NaOCl}\\) solution, because the weaker parent acid \\(\\text{HClO}\\) furnishes the stronger conjugate base \\(\\text{OCl}^-\\), giving it the larger \\(K_b\\)."},{"label":"D","text":"Neither; both are salts of a weak acid and a strong base, so both anions hydrolyze to the same extent and the two solutions reach equal \\(\\text{pH}\\)."}]$op57$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex57$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is C because the basicity of a salt of a weak acid and a strong base is set by the conjugate-base strength of the anion, and conjugate-base strength runs inverse to the strength of the parent acid through the relation \(K_a \times K_b = K_w\). Rearranged, \(K_b = K_w / K_a\), so the anion of the weaker acid has the larger \(K_b\). Here \(\text{HClO}\) (\(K_a = 2.9\times10^{-8}\)) is the weaker of the two parent acids, so \(\text{OCl}^-\) is the stronger conjugate base: \(K_b(\text{OCl}^-) = 1.0\times10^{-14} / 2.9\times10^{-8} = 3.4\times10^{-7}\). For \(\text{NO}_2^-\), \(K_b = 1.0\times10^{-14} / 4.6\times10^{-4} = 2.2\times10^{-11}\). Because \(K_b(\text{OCl}^-) \approx 3.4\times10^{-7}\) is about four orders of magnitude larger than \(K_b(\text{NO}_2^-) \approx 2.2\times10^{-11}\), \(\text{OCl}^-\) hydrolyzes far more extensively at the same \(0.10\ \text{M}\), producing a higher \([\text{OH}^-]\) and therefore a higher \(\text{pH}\). The \(\text{NaOCl}\) solution is the more basic. (Choice A) The amount of anion delivered is fixed by the dissolved salt concentration (\(0.10\ \text{M}\)) and the complete dissociation of the strong-base salt, not by the parent acid's \(K_a\); a larger parent \(K_a\) does not put more \(\text{NO}_2^-\) into solution, and in any case it would mean weaker, not stronger, hydrolysis. (Choice B) This reverses the inverse relationship between an acid and its conjugate base: a stronger parent acid holds its proton less tightly only in the dissociation sense, but its conjugate base is correspondingly weaker (smaller \(K_b\)), so \(\text{HNO}_2\) being the stronger acid makes \(\text{NO}_2^-\) the weaker base, not the stronger one. (Choice D) Both anions do come from weak acids and so both make basic solutions, but the extent of hydrolysis is governed by each anion's \(K_b\), and these differ by roughly \(10^4\); the two solutions cannot reach the same \(\text{pH}\). Skill 4$ex57$,
    'hard', '5A', $cc57$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc57$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
