-- Q26 [Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions · hard · Skill 2 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans D] (g58)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions',
    $qt58$Two salts are each dissolved to \(0.10\ \text{M}\) in separate beakers of water: ammonium chloride (\(\text{NH}_4\text{Cl}\), whose parent base \(\text{NH}_3\) has \(K_b = 1.8\times10^{-5}\)) and anilinium chloride (\(\text{C}_6\text{H}_5\text{NH}_3\text{Cl}\), whose parent base aniline has \(K_b = 4.3\times10^{-10}\)). In both salts the chloride is a spectator. Which solution is more acidic, and why?$qt58$,
    $op58$[{"label":"A","text":"The \\(\\text{NH}_4\\text{Cl}\\) solution, because \\(\\text{NH}_3\\) has the larger \\(K_b\\), so its cation \\(\\text{NH}_4^+\\) is the stronger conjugate acid."},{"label":"B","text":"The \\(\\text{C}_6\\text{H}_5\\text{NH}_3\\text{Cl}\\) solution, because the chloride from aniline's salt makes the solution acidic while ammonium's chloride is neutral."},{"label":"C","text":"Neither; both cations come from weak bases, so the two solutions reach the same pH at equal concentration."},{"label":"D","text":"The \\(\\text{C}_6\\text{H}_5\\text{NH}_3\\text{Cl}\\) solution, because aniline's smaller \\(K_b\\) gives anilinium the larger \\(K_a\\) via \\(K_a = K_w/K_b\\)."}]$op58$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex58$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is D because the more acidic salt solution is the one whose cation is the stronger conjugate acid, and conjugate-acid strength is set by \(K_a = K_w/K_b\) of the parent base. For ammonium, \(K_a(\text{NH}_4^+) = K_w/K_b = (1.0\times10^{-14})/(1.8\times10^{-5}) = 5.6\times10^{-10}\). For anilinium, \(K_a(\text{C}_6\text{H}_5\text{NH}_3^+) = (1.0\times10^{-14})/(4.3\times10^{-10}) = 2.3\times10^{-5}\). Because \(K_a\) and \(K_b\) are inversely related, the weaker parent base (aniline, with the smaller \(K_b\)) gives the stronger conjugate-acid cation: anilinium's \(K_a\) is about \(4\times10^{4}\) times larger than ammonium's. At equal \(0.10\ \text{M}\) concentration the cation with the larger \(K_a\) hydrolyzes more extensively, producing a higher \([\text{H}^+]\) and lower \(\text{pH}\), so the anilinium chloride solution is more acidic. (Choice A) This reverses the relationship: a larger parent-base \(K_b\) means a weaker, not stronger, conjugate acid, since \(K_a = K_w/K_b\) is inversely proportional to \(K_b\). (Choice B) Chloride is the conjugate base of the strong acid \(\text{HCl}\) in both salts and is a spectator in both, so it contributes nothing to acidity; it cannot distinguish the two solutions. (Choice C) Equal concentration does not give equal pH here, because the two cations have very different \(K_a\) values (\(2.3\times10^{-5}\) versus \(5.6\times10^{-10}\)), and the cation with the larger \(K_a\) yields the lower pH. Skill 2$ex58$,
    'hard', '5A', $cc58$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc58$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
